ctlStampT -- P4: THE CONTROL SIGNAL IS NOT STAMPED ON THE RETURNED VALUE.

Before P4, aCTionBrancH wrote isBranch = 1/2/3 (break/continue/return) onto whatever it
returned -- a live field, falseResult, the keyword node. P4 (Tony, SEQ 179) moves the kind
to a ruler slot, `branchKind`: ONE writer (aCTionBrancH), read by the firing parent after
each child (BlocK, DO, FOR, WhilE, IF, each clearing it before the child), and saved,
cleared and restored at three frames -- processAction, parseRule's fire of a generated
body, and the jit's emit walk. Nothing writes isBranch any more; the read-only isBrancH
accessor (GroupFields 45) watches it stay 0.

THE CERTIFICATE (SEQ 194), WITH ITS CONTROLS -- the pre-P4 readings, measured 2026-09-26 on
parse-then-fire at e181681 (stamp intact, accessor present), incant/ctlStampProbe:
  P4-1  `continue v;` leaves v's isBranch        want 0    CONTROL (pre-P4)  2
  P4-1s the loop over it ran                     want 2    CONTROL           2
  P4-2  testing()-compiled `return w;` w's        want 0    CONTROL           3
The two rows the plan first named -- `return x;` and a failed operand's falseResult -- read
0 BEFORE P4 as well (processAction cleared the returned value's stamp), so they could not
discriminate. That is why the certificate is the two SURVIVING stamps.

THE PER-BRACKET ROWS (SEQ 194). P4 leaves NO stamp for a bracket to clear, so by ruling
they assert the returned value carries no control stamp:
  LV-1  processAction: `return x;` hands back 7, x's isBranch 0
  LV-2  parseRule -- NO ROW, and the reason is measured: every generated body ends
        `return <expr>;`, but parseRule consumes that value and returns the LABEL, so the
        returned value never leaves the bracket. With the stamp put back (H7 control) the
        three spellings tried -- `true`, a captured `1 && 1`, tell's reply -- all read 0,
        so a row there would be green and certify nothing. SL-2 carries this bracket.
  (the emit frame's is P4-2: w is the operand of the compiled return)

Those rows cannot fail if a bracket is REMOVED (there is no stamp to leak), so each bracket
also carries a SLOT-LEAK row, which does (H7):
  SL-1  processAction: a loop over an action that returns runs all 3
  SL-2  parseRule: a loop over tell() drives of a generated rule runs all 3
  SL-3  emit frame: the statement after testing() in the same block runs (ctM 5)
H7, MEASURED 2026-09-26, each control on this fixture alone, rebuilt bare after:
  stamp put back (arg.isBranch = branchKind):  P4-1 2, P4-2 3, LV-1 x 3 -- all red
  processAction restore removed:  ctRun stops after P4-2; LV-1 and SL-1..3 VANISH
  parseRule restore removed:      SL-2 3 -> 0, nothing else moves
  emit-frame restore removed:     SL-3 5 -> 0, nothing else moves
ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING: ctEmitLeak runs FIRST so testing() compiles ctJit before anything
